WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.503
Authorization determines what authenticated subjects can do to protected resources under specific conditions.

2
00:00:07.503 --> 00:00:15.161
It is different from authentication, and access decisions should never be trusted merely because a user is logged in.

3
00:00:15.161 --> 00:00:23.747
Subjects, resources, actions, and context give teams a practical language for describing who can do what, to which object, and why.

4
00:00:23.747 --> 00:00:37.206
Strong authorization depends on explicit models, least privilege, default deny, separation of duties, object-level checks, tenant boundaries, server-side enforcement, and safe policy architecture.

5
00:00:37.206 --> 00:00:43.085
RBAC, ABAC, ReBAC, scopes, claims, groups, roles, and entitlements are tools for design.

6
00:00:43.085 --> 00:00:50.588
They work only when the application uses them consistently and reviewers can understand the resulting decisions.

7
00:00:50.588 --> 00:00:57.241
APIs, services, workloads, and delegated access need the same level of care as human user interfaces.

8
00:00:57.241 --> 00:01:00.876
Internal traffic should not automatically be trusted.

9
00:01:00.876 --> 00:01:10.932
Gateways can help, but application-layer enforcement still matters where business objects, tenant boundaries, and sensitive actions are understood.

10
00:01:10.932 --> 00:01:15.651
Authorization context must be carried safely across services and jobs.

11
00:01:15.651 --> 00:01:18.049
The goal is not simply to block users.

12
00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:24.469
The goal is predictable, appropriate, auditable access aligned with business and security risk.

13
00:01:24.469 --> 00:01:31.198
Testing, logging, monitoring, governance, and review keep authorization healthy as systems change.

14
00:01:31.198 --> 00:01:37.387
Mature authorization programs make access clear enough to operate and strong enough to trust.
